---
// STUB — picks up once v1.4 (Astro substrate) merges.
//
// This page hosts the Pick Your Agent Stack island.
// Data source: src/data/stack-questions.json (10 questions, 8 named stacks).
//
// Implementation TODO (post v1.4 merge):
// 1. Import the JSON: `import questions from '../data/stack-questions.json';`
// 2. Render the <PickStack /> island (see src/components/PickStack.astro).
// 3. URL-encode answers in the hash (e.g. `#latency=async&isolation=microvm&...`)
//    so users can share a stack with a one-line URL.
// 4. The island sums weights, picks the highest-scoring stack, and
//    renders the "why not the alternatives" block from `loser_rationale`.
// 5. Add a "Share this stack" button that copies the current URL.
// 6. Add SEO: title, description, og:image (static at first; the report
//    PDF pipeline will eventually render a per-result og:image).
//
// Acceptance criteria (from PLAN.md):
// - 8-12 questions (we ship 10) covering: latency, isolation, language, budget,
//   MCP support, sync vs async, scale, compliance, license, observability.
// - 6-10 named stacks (we ship 8) with rationale + "why not" text.
// - Shareable URL encodes answers.
// - Every recommendation shows "why not the 7 alternatives" with loser_rationale.

import Layout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PickStack from '../components/PickStack.astro';
import questions from '../data/stack-questions.json';
---

<Layout
  title="Pick Your Agent Stack"
  description="Ten questions → a concrete stack recommendation: inference, sandbox, orchestrator, observability, memory. Plus why every alternative loses in your case."
>
  <main>
    <h1>Pick Your Agent Stack</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Ten questions. One opinionated recommendation. Eight loser rationales.
      Share the URL to argue with the inputs, not the output.
    </p>

    <PickStack questions={questions.questions} stacks={questions.stacks} />

    <section class="meta">
      <h2>How this works</h2>
      <p>
        Every answer carries a weight toward each named stack. We sum the
        weights and pick the highest-scoring stack. The dataset is checked
        into the repo at
        <code>src/data/stack-questions.json</code> — propose changes via PR.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
